In this Part, (a) “assessment officer” means the court clerk for the judicial centre in which the action is located;
(b) “review officer” means an assessment officer who, in the opinion of the clerk of the Court, has for the purpose of reviewing contingency fee or retainer agreements and lawyers’ charges (i) an acceptable degree in law, and (ii) sufficient experience in the practice of law, and who is desig- nated as a review officer by [Alta. Reg. 124/2010, r. 10.1 effective November 1, 2010 (Alta. Gaz. May 15, 2021)]
Subdivision 1: LAWYERS’ CHARGES

commentary.text:
The purposes of rule 10.10 are as follows:
• give the client a fair opportunity to challenge a retainer agreement and/or accounts;
• put lawyers on notice that a client has concerns in case they have an ongoing relationship;
• encourage clients to voice concerns close to the date they arise, which allows both parties to retain records and make notes relevant to the concerns, and helps prevent the difficulty of assessing complaints years after difficulties may have occurred;
• allow lawyers to anticipate uncollectible receivables, or to know that collected accounts may have to be returned, within a reasonable period of time;
• allow lawyers to have finality of accounts. Rath and Co. Barristers and Solicitors v. Sturgeon Lake Cree Nation, [2019] A.J. No. 1688, 2019 ABQB 949 at para. 16 (Alta. Q.B. (Master)), revd on other grounds [2022] A.J. No. 996, 2022 ABQB The former version of this rule only applied to client-initiated reviews. Twinn v. Sawridge Band, [2012] A.J. No. 59, 2012 ABQB 44 at paras. 34-40 (Alta. Q.B.). The words “whether at the request of the lawyer or the client” were added to the rule on July 25, 2013.
Lawyers are now bound by the rule. O’Brien v. de Villars Jones, [2015] A.J. No. 918, 2015 ABQB 535 at para. 6 (Alta. Q.B. (Master)). The rule does not apply to interim invoices, only final invoices. O’Brien v. de Villars Jones, [2015] A.J. No. 918, 2015 ABQB 535 at para. 8 (Alta. Q.B. (Master)). For whether an account is interim or final, see , [2013] A.J. Samson Cree Nation v. O’Reilly & Associés No. 670, 2013 ABQB 350 at paras. 45, 46 (Alta. Q.B.), affd [2014] A.J. No. 904, 2014 ABCA 268 (Alta. C.A.).
